{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Review-plan run 1 BLOCKED, and the blocking finding is correct. The binding walk's level-triggered ADOPT arm (binding_walk._adopt_stands_down) already replays exactly this orphan: a lone rebar-id marker equal to the deterministic inbound id, no binding, no local ticket. My reproduction used a 1-binding store, so the blast-radius breaker refused that ADOPT (1/1 > 0.10) on every pass. Measured: with 1 and 9 confirmed bindings the orphan is held (breaker refuses; logged as binding_walk_breaker_refused); with 20 it is imported on the next pass with one CREATE, a binding, no create_issue and a single label, and later passes make no writes. Production has 5953 confirmed bindings, so a failed pass would need more than 595 orphaned imports before the breaker holds them, and holding is the breaker's designed, logged behaviour. Also measured: inbound applies before outbound, so a refused inbound write ends the pass before any outbound Jira write, and plain inbound changes are re-applied next pass. Conclusion: delayed, not lost or duplicated. The fix in this plan is not needed (it would also bypass the breaker). The premise is false; the proof lands as a test under feministic-baboonish-snake, and this bug closes not_a_bug citing it."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1795271786229889001,"uuid":"a102a3ae-846e-4a15-93da-e423734867e3"}